4. Authentication
ReqRes uses an API key for requests under the /api/ path. The API key was stored in a Postman environment variable named api_key and referenced by requests using {{api_key}}.
The Login endpoint was also tested using POST /api/login. A successful login response returns a bearer token. The token was automatically stored in the Postman environment as auth_token for the authentication demonstration.
[ SCREENSHOT: Login request and successful 200 response ]

5. Postman Environment Variables
The Postman environment used for the testing collection was named ReqRes Env.
Variable
Example / Value
Purpose
base_url
https://reqres.in/api
Stores the API base URL
auth_token
Auto-populated after Login
Stores the returned login token
api_key
ReqRes API key
Used for API-key authentication
user_id
2
Stores the user ID used by user-specific requests

Sensitive values such as the API key and bearer token should be hidden or removed before publishing screenshots, exported environments, or the project repository.
[ SCREENSHOT: Postman environment (API key/token masked) ]



6. CRUD Testing
The following HTTP methods were tested in Postman: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE. The /api/users requests used the api_key environment variable for API-key authentication. The bearer token obtained from the Login endpoint was automatically stored in the environment as part of the authentication demonstration.
6.1 GET – Retrieve Users
GET /users?page=2 was used to retrieve a list of users and returned HTTP 200 OK. A single-user request, GET /users/{id}, was also tested successfully with a valid user ID.
[ SCREENSHOT: GET All Users and GET Single User ]






6.2 POST – Create User
POST /users was used to create a test user. The request returned HTTP 201 Created and the response included generated user information, an ID and a timestamp.
[ SCREENSHOT: Create User response ]

6.3 PUT – Full Update
PUT /users/{id} was used to perform a full update of a test user's data. The request returned HTTP 200 OK.
[ SCREENSHOT: PUT Update User response ]

6.4 PATCH – Partial Update
PATCH /users/{id} was used to update only the job field. The request returned HTTP 200 OK and the response confirmed the updated value.
Example request body:
{
  "job": "Senior QA Engineer"
}
[ SCREENSHOT: PATCH Update User response ]

6.5 DELETE – Delete User
DELETE /users/{id} was used to delete a test user. The request returned HTTP 204 No Content, which indicates that the request was successfully processed without a response body.
[ SCREENSHOT: DELETE User response ]

7. Automated Scripts
Postman scripts were used to automate authentication-related setup and response validation.
7.1 Pre-request Script
A Pre-request Script was added to the Login request to automatically insert the current API key from the Postman environment before the request is sent.
pm.request.headers.upsert({
    key: "x-api-key",
    value: pm.environment.get("api_key")
});
7.2 After-response Token Storage
After a successful Login response, the returned token was stored automatically in the auth_token variable.
pm.environment.set("auth_token", pm.response.json().token);

8. Automated Tests
Login tests:
pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response has token", function () {
    var jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.token).to.exist;
});
PATCH tests:
pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});

pm.test("Response contains updated job", function () {
    var jsonData = pm.response.json();
    pm.expect(jsonData.job).to.eql("Senior QA Engineer");
});
9. Collection Runner
The complete Postman collection was executed using the Collection Runner. The final request order was:
Login
GET All Users
GET Single User
Create User
PUT Update User
PATCH Update User
DELETE User
The final Runner execution completed successfully after correcting the user ID used by the single-user request and ensuring the Login tests were present. The final Runner result is shown below.
